+/workspace/conversations/14433486b5bb4cda850c52dd19003e19/events/event-00046-c2e12a9c-3003-496a-a30f-4559df9278d2.json:1:{"id":"c2e12a9c-3003-496a-a30f-4559df9278d2","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:00:10.595149","source":"agent","parent_id":"151164fc-9cad-4a89-8b38-db50104c243e","thought":[],"thinking_blocks":[],"action":{"command":"grep -rn \"ots\\|check.anchors\" /workspace/conversations /workspace/runs /workspace/verifications 2>/dev/null | head -30","is_input":false,"reset":false,"kind":"TerminalAction"},"tool_name":"terminal","tool_call_id":"call_8bec50365f814573b3a8a541","tool_call":{"id":"call_8bec50365f814573b3a8a541","name":"terminal","arguments":"{\"command\": \"grep -rn \\\"ots\\\\|check.anchors\\\" /workspace/conversations /workspace/runs /workspace/verifications 2>/dev/null | head -30\"}","origin":"completion"},"llm_response_id":"gen-1794383997-rZ2HY0CeUy0ja9AyPTOI","security_risk":"UNKNOWN","summary":"terminal: {\"command\": \"grep -rn \\\"ots\\\\|check.anchors\\\" /workspace/conversations /workspace/runs /workspace/verifications 2>/dev/null | head -30\"}","kind":"ActionEvent"}
+/workspace/conversations/14433486b5bb4cda850c52dd19003e19/events/event-00042-9e9ef3a5-b33f-4f3c-8f09-049a79f7a5de.json:1:{"id":"9e9ef3a5-b33f-4f3c-8f09-049a79f7a5de","timestamp":"2026-11-11T07:59:57.031622","source":"environment","parent_id":"c54bf18b-9c36-4d19-b43c-418454101547","tool_name":"terminal","tool_call_id":"call_2b73063f0296421c9577a56c","observation":{"content":[{"cache_prompt":false,"type":"text","text":"1:\"\"\"Tests for record.py's ledger rules (offline; anchors are tested in CI).\"\"\"\n73:    def test_dropped_entry_with_rewritten_chain_is_only_caught_by_anchors(self):\n75:        # the chain alone can't show a missing record. That's what anchors and the monitor are for.\n218:    def test_check_anchors_requires_the_writer_to_cover_every_entry(self):\n221:        rc, out, err = self.run_cmd(\"check-anchors\")\n---\n- **one verdict per plan:** a re-run needs a new plan, so re-running until a result looks good shows up as several plans;\n- a verdict's `by` differs from its plan's `by`. Handles are claims, not identities.\n\n`verify` lists **open** and **overdue** plans. Completeness can't come from cryptography, so this list is how a missing verdict becomes visible.\n\n**Orphan entries.** An attestation the writer made but the ledger doesn't hold (a run that failed or was cancelled after attesting) is flagged by `monitor` until an `orphan` entry `{by, log_index, reason, statement_b64, entries_b64}` explains it.\n\nThe orphan entry must **publish what the run attested**: the exact statement, plus the hidden entries themselves. The entries must be canonical, consecutive from the attested `from_seq`, chained from the attested `prev_sha256`, and hash to the attested head. The explanation is refused if:\n- the statement isn't the one Rekor holds for that log index (its payload hash);\n- the entries don't reproduce the attested range;\n- the attested head was ever published on the ledger branch and later removed (needs a full clone).\n\nSo hiding an attested verdict means publishing it inside the orphan entry. The `reason` is a claim; the entries are the evidence.\n\n## What a stranger can check, and how\n| Question | How |\n|---|---|\n| Entry unchanged since anchored? | `check-anchors`: Sigstore subject and OTS proof match its bytes |\n| Every entry vouched for by the writer? | `check-anchors`: ranges cover 1..head, no gap or overlap |\n| Existed by when? | Rekor `integratedTime`; Bitcoin block time (existence only) |\n| Which code wrote each range? | `check-anchors`: each run's certified commit, and whether anything under `record/` or `.github/` changed since the last run |\n| Runs held back, forked or deleted? | `monitor`: every writer attestation on any ref is in the ledger or published by an orphan entry; no force push or deletion of the ledger branch |\n| Plans without a verdict? | `verify`: open and overdue plans |\n\n**Verify an anchor without this code:**\n- **Sigstore:**\n  ```\n  sigstore verify github --bundle ledger/anchors/N.sigstore.json \\\n    --cert-identity https://github.com/5kasgard/exagium/.github/workflows/record.yml@refs/heads/main \\\n    --repository 5kasgard/exagium --ref refs/heads/main sha256:<sha256 of entry N's file>\n  ```\n- **With `gh`:**\n  ```\n  gh attestation verify ledger/entries/N.json -R 5kasgard/exagium \\\n    --signer-workflow 5kasgard/exagium/.github/workflows/record.yml --source-ref refs/heads/main \\\n    --predicate-type https://github.com/5kasgard/exagium/blob/main/record/README.md\n  ```\n  Never with `-R` alone (Q1 below).\n- **OpenTimestamps:** `ots verify` with a Bitcoin node, or the opentimestamps.org web page from a phone.\n\n## Experiments (2026-11-05/06)\nAll ran on GitHub Actions with test copies of the writer, each with its own identity. The test copy kept on this branch is `.github/workflows/record-e2e.yml`, whose `append` job is identical to `record.yml`'s apart from names.\n\n**Q1: does `gh attestation verify -R repo` accept other workflows and branches?** Yes.\n- With `-R` alone, it accepted an attestation that `anchor-test.yml` made on `refs/heads/anchor-test`.\n- Adding `--signer-workflow` or `--source-ref` refused it (`ci-runs` `q1/42092874356.txt`).\n- Positive control: pinned to the right writer and ref, `gh` accepts the genuine e2e anchors; pinned to the wrong writer, it refuses (`ci-runs` `e2e/*.txt`, 6 runs).\n- A predicate type containing `#` is rejected by GitHub's API (HTTP 422), so ours has none.\n\n**Q2: does the monitor catch a run the owner never publishes?** Yes.\n- **Setup:** a writer copy (`record-q2.yml`, since retired) attested a \"hidden\" entry on a shadow branch, and the run never published it.\n- **Result:** `monitor`, reading only the stand-in main's ledger, flagged it (exit 1). It also flagged two earlier failed attempts that had attested before failing. Those weren't planted: even aborted runs leave a public trace.\n- **Branch deletion** isn't tested, because this session can't delete branches. GitHub's activity log, which `monitor` reads by cursor, records creation and deletion.\n\n**End to end** (`e2e-main`, 8 entries, ranges 1–1, 2–3, 4–5, 6–7, 8–8):\n- **A, first entry:** written, attested, checked.\n- **B, a hand-written entry pushed with a proposal:** the writer refused at its pre-check. Nothing was attested (run 42095360021), and the monitor later found no stray attestation.\n- **C and D, two racing pushes:** one run took both proposals, and the other found an empty inbox.\n- **E, two racing pushes with the final writer:** the older run saw `main` had moved and stepped aside. The newer one ran its own certified commit (`a431e68`) and took both. `check-anchors` reports the writer code changed before entries 6–7, which is true.\n- **F, the final writer** (pinned actions, `python3 -I`): entry 8, run 42098215091.\n- **Final state:** `check-anchors` exits 0 (writer attested through 8; code changed before 6–7 and before 8, both true). `monitor` exits 0: 5 Rekor entries, 5 anchors, 6 refs, no force push or deletion.\n\n## Review\nThree rounds by one independent adversarial agent.\n\n**Round 1 found three demonstrated breaks:**\n1. **Hand-written entries passed every check,** and the next CI run would vouch for them. Fixed by range attestations, full coverage, and the writer's pre-check.\n2. **The monitor stayed red forever** because of old orphans. Fixed by orphan entries.\n3. **Activity paging was broken** (the API pages by cursor). Fixed.\n\nIt also found:"}],"is_error":false,"command":"grep -n \"anchor\" /workspace/record/test_record.py; echo ---; sed -n 60,130p /workspace/record/README.md","exit_code":0,"timeout":false,"metadata":{"exit_code":0,"pid":-1,"username":"openhands","hostname":"9951823ab17e","working_dir":"/workspace","py_interpreter_path":"/usr/local/bin/python","prefix":"","suffix":"\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]"},"full_output_save_dir":"workspace/conversations/14433486b5bb4cda850c52dd19003e19/observations","kind":"TerminalObservation"},"action_id":"93f06443-a246-4e5b-97c0-ca7b8d1a3c27","extended_content":[],"kind":"ObservationEvent"}
+/workspace/conversations/14433486b5bb4cda850c52dd19003e19/events/event-00043-a633c3aa-c7a9-4322-a9a7-6feb79bda2c0.json:1:{"id":"a633c3aa-c7a9-4322-a9a7-6feb79bda2c0","timestamp":"2026-11-11T07:59:57.172734","source":"environment","parent_id":"9e9ef3a5-b33f-4f3c-8f09-049a79f7a5de","tool_name":"terminal","tool_call_id":"call_a15d6a5ce63c4edda1161a3a","observation":{"content":[{"cache_prompt":false,"type":"text","text":"- name: step aside if the branch has moved on\n        id: current\n        run: |\n          git fetch -q origin \"$LEDGER_REF\"\n          if [ \"$(git rev-parse FETCH_HEAD)\" = \"$GITHUB_SHA\" ]; then echo \"go=yes\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"; else echo \"superseded by $(git rev-parse FETCH_HEAD)\"; echo \"go=no\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"; fi\n      - name: tools\n        if: steps.current.outputs.go == 'yes'\n        run: |\n          # an isolated virtualenv: the runner's system pyOpenSSL breaks under a newer cryptography\n          python3 -m venv \"$RUNNER_TEMP/venv\" && \"$RUNNER_TEMP/venv/bin/pip\" install -q sigstore==4.5.1 opentimestamps-client==0.8.0\n          echo \"$RUNNER_TEMP/venv/bin\" >> \"$GITHUB_PATH\"\n      - name: the existing chain must be fully attested by this writer before anything is added\n        if: steps.current.outputs.go == 'yes'\n        run: |\n          # exit 2 (an explorer unreachable) still means every entry is covered: coverage comes from Sigstore, checked offline\n          rc=0; python3 -I record/record.py check-anchors --ref \"refs/heads/$LEDGER_REF\" --workflow \"$WRITER\" || rc=$?\n          [ $rc -eq 0 ] || [ $rc -eq 2 ]\n      - name: ingest inbox into the chain\n        if: steps.current.outputs.go == 'yes'\n        id: ingest\n        run: |\n          read before _ < <(python3 -I record/record.py head)\n          python3 -I record/record.py ingest | tee \"$RUNNER_TEMP/written.txt\"\n          read seq sha < <(python3 -I record/record.py head)\n          echo \"from=$((before + 1))\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"; echo \"seq=$seq\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"; echo \"sha=$sha\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n          echo \"file=ledger/entries/$(printf %06d $seq).json\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n          echo \"n=$(wc -l < \"$RUNNER_TEMP/written.txt\")\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n          echo \"predicate=$(python3 -I record/record.py range $((before + 1)) $seq 2>/dev/null || echo none)\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n      - name: stamp the head (OpenTimestamps); refuse to attest if the branch moved\n        if: steps.current.outputs.go == 'yes' && steps.ingest.outputs.n != '0'\n        run: |\n          s=$(printf %06d ${{ steps.ingest.outputs.seq }})\n          mkdir -p ledger/anchors\n          cp ledger/entries/$s.json \"$RUNNER_TEMP/$s.json\" && ots stamp \"$RUNNER_TEMP/$s.json\"\n          mv \"$RUNNER_TEMP/$s.json.ots\" ledger/anchors/$s.ots\n          python3 -I record/record.py verify\n          git fetch -q origin \"$LEDGER_REF\"\n          [ \"$(git rev-parse FETCH_HEAD)\" = \"$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" ] || { echo \"the branch moved since checkout; not attesting\"; exit 1; }\n      - name: attest the range of entries added (Sigstore, public Rekor)\n        if: steps.current.outputs.go == 'yes' && steps.ingest.outputs.n != '0'\n        id: att\n        uses: actions/attest@ce27ba3b4a9a139d9a20a4a07d69fabb52f1e5bc  # v2.4.0\n        with:\n          subject-path: ${{ steps.ingest.outputs.file }}\n          predicate-type: https://github.com/5kasgard/exagium/blob/main/record/README.md\n          predicate: ${{ steps.ingest.outputs.predicate }}\n      - name: check every anchor and commit\n        if: steps.current.outputs.go == 'yes' && steps.ingest.outputs.n != '0'\n        run: |\n          s=$(printf %06d ${{ steps.ingest.outputs.seq }})\n          cp \"${{ steps.att.outputs.bundle-path }}\" ledger/anchors/$s.sigstore.json\n          # exit 2 (an explorer unreachable) mustn't orphan the attestation just made; exit 1 or 3 stops here\n          rc=0; python3 -I record/record.py check-anchors --ref \"refs/heads/$LEDGER_REF\" --workflow \"$WRITER\" || rc=$?\n          [ $rc -eq 0 ] || [ $rc -eq 2 ]\n          git config user.name github-actions\n          git config user.email 41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com\n          git add ledger\n          git commit -q -m \"ledger: entries ${{ steps.ingest.outputs.from }}-${{ steps.ingest.outputs.seq }}, head sha256 ${{ steps.ingest.outputs.sha }} (run ${{ github.run_id }})\"\n          git push -q origin \"HEAD:$LEDGER_REF\"\n      - name: after a failure past attesting, keep the orphaned attestation's bundle and entries on ci-runs\n        if: (failure() || cancelled()) && steps.att.outcome == 'success'\n        run: |\n          d=\"$RUNNER_TEMP/ci\"; git init -q \"$d\"\n          cp -r ledger \"$RUNNER_TEMP/ledger-orphan\"; cp \"${{ steps.att.outputs.bundle-path }}\" \"$RUNNER_TEMP/ledger-orphan/bundle.sigstore.json\"\n          cd \"$d\" && git remote add origin \"https://x-access-token:${{ github.token }}@github.com/${{ github.repository }}\"\n          git fetch -q origin ci-runs && git checkout -q ci-runs\n   <response clipped><NOTE>Due to the max output limit, only part of the full response has been shown to you.</NOTE>heck the corresponding .ots file exists (for range head seq). Should the .ots check be part of the loop or a separate pass? Simplest: within the sigstore branch, before/after check_sigstore, check os.path.exists of the .ots path; if not, raise Bad(f\"{seq:06d}.ots is missing\")? But raising Bad from within the try would go to bad — good, exit 1. But message: \"must print a message to stderr containing NNNNNN.ots is missing\". If we raise Bad(f\"{n}: {seq:06d}.ots is missing\") the stderr shows \"BAD: anchors/000002.sigstore.json: 000002.ots is missing\" — contains \"000002.ots is missing\". \n\nBut also should missing .ots be checked even for the sigstore case only? What if there is no sigstore anchor but an .ots anchor exists? The .ots anchor itself is iterated, and check_ots would fail with Bad (open missing -> FileNotFoundError, which isn't caught → exit 3). Hmm, check_ots opens the proof; if missing, FileNotFoundError propagates → exit 3 (unexpected failure). That's a bug too maybe, but the reported bug is about deleted proof of range head covered by sigstore.\n\nBetter approach: add a missing-file check in the loop. Since _listdir already validates files in the dir, deleted file means not listed.\n\nPlan:\n1. In cmd_check_anchors loop, for each anchor, determine whether a corresponding .ots for the range head is expected. For a sigstore anchor at seq, expected ots name is f\"{seq:06d}.ots\" and it should exist (writer writes both). For an ots anchor at seq, its existence is the proof itself.\n\nActually, what does the writer do? Layout: anchors/NNNNNN.sigstore.json attests range ending at NNNNNN, and anchors/NNNNNN.ots is the proof of entry NNNNNN's bytes. Presumably both written per run; sigstore range head NNNNNN has ots proof for NNNNNN. So for each anchor listed, if kind is sigstore, expect the .ots for seq to exist.\n\nSimplest minimal fix: before checking the sigstore bundle, check that the ots proof file exists; if not, add Bad f\"{seq:06d}.ots is missing\". Or do it after the loop for all sigstore anchors. Within the loop is cleaner.\n\nBut also the case where the .ots anchor file itself is missing while... no, deletion means both? The bug: deleted anchors/000002.ots, anchors/000002.sigstore.json remains. So fix: for each anchor path processed, check the sibling ots.\n\nShould we also check that an ots anchor at seq is consistent... The existing behavior for present ots is fine.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```python\n            if m.group(2) == \"ots\":\n                ...\n            else:\n                ots = os.path.join(a.ledger, \"anchors\", f\"{seq:06d}.ots\")\n                if not os.path.isfile(ots):\n                    raise Bad(f\"{seq:06d}.ots is missing\")\n                ...\n```\n\nWait: message should contain \"NNNNNN.ots is missing\" — f\"{seq:06d}.ots is missing\" gives \"000002.ots is missing\". Good. But should it be prefixed with anchors/? Requirement: \"print a message to stderr containing NNNNNN.ots is missing (for example 000002.ots is missing)\". The Bad handler prints \"BAD: {ex}\". So message like f\"anchors/{n}: {seq:06d}.ots is missing\" would contain it too, and it's informative. Either works. I'll include context: raise Bad(f\"anchors/{n}: {seq:06d}.ots is missing\").\n\nHmm, but maybe better to not rely on the sigstore file existing: any entry the writer attested via sigstore should have a proof. Also what about the case where only .ots anchors exist (no sigstore)? Then the ots file itself is in the loop and check_ots handles it — but if it's missing, _listdir wouldn't list it at all, and nothing would catch it. E.g., if all anchors were .ots and one deleted — the deletion just means it's not in the listing, and the coverage check would catch \"never attested\". Actually with ots-only anchors, deleting the .ots means the range isn't attested → caught by coverage check. With sigstore, the sigstore attests ranges, and ots missing is caught by our new check.\n\nBut wait — should we also require the .ots for a range head when the head was covered by an ots anchor? The coverage check uses ranges from sigstore only. If the writer used ots anchors... Let me check how CI writes anchors, and the ledger dir.\n\nAlso there's a subtlety: what about entries covered by sigstore but where the head anchor... The required behavior: \"check-anchors must exit 1 when the .ots proof for any attested range head is missing.\" So: for each sigstore anchor (attested range ending at seq), the .ots proof for that range head (seq) must exist.\n\nLet me check the ledger directory and CI workflow for context.","thinking_blocks":[],"action":{"command":"ls -R /workspace/ledger | head -40; ls /workspace/ledger/anchors 2>/dev/null; grep -rn \"ots\" /workspace/.github/workflows/*.yml